For your Fake Currency Detection using Deep Learning project, you can use a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to train a model that analyzes currency images and predicts whether an image belongs to the genuine or counterfeit class.
1. Model training workflow
1. Collect currency images
Genuine and counterfeit images with verified labels

2. Preprocess images
Resize images and normalize pixel values

3. Split the dataset
Training, validation and testing sets

4. Train the CNN model
Learn visual patterns from labeled images

5. Evaluate and save the model
Measure performance on unseen test images


2. Training code for Jupyter Notebook
First, organize your image dataset like this:
dataset/
├── real/
│   ├── real1.jpg
│   ├── real2.jpg
│   └── ...
└── fake/
    ├── fake1.jpg
    ├── fake2.jpg
    └── ...


Then run these notebook cells in order. This uses TensorFlow/Keras image classification, following the official TensorFlow image classification tutorial. 

tensorflow.org
+1



Cell 1 — Import libraries
import tensorflow as tfimport numpy as npimport matplotlib.pyplot as pltprint("TensorFlow version:", tf.__version__)



Cell 2 — Load and split the dataset
Replace the folder path with the location of your dataset.
DATASET_PATH = r"C:\Users\indus\Downloads\currency_dataset"IMG_SIZE = (224, 224)BATCH_SIZE = 16train_ds = tf.keras.utils.image_dataset_from_directory(    DATASET_PATH,    validation_split=0.2,    subset="training",    seed=42,    image_size=IMG_SIZE,    batch_size=BATCH_SIZE,    class_names=["fake", "real"])val_ds = tf.keras.utils.image_dataset_from_directory(    DATASET_PATH,    validation_split=0.2,    subset="validation",    seed=42,    image_size=IMG_SIZE,    batch_size=BATCH_SIZE,    class_names=["fake", "real"])print("Class names:", train_ds.class_names)



This creates training and validation sets. Keep a separate test dataset for the final evaluation if possible.
Cell 3 — Build the CNN model
model = tf.keras.Sequential([    tf.keras.layers.Input(shape=(224, 224, 3)),    tf.keras.layers.Rescaling(1./255),    tf.keras.layers.Conv2D(32, (3, 3), activation="relu"),    tf.keras.layers.MaxPooling2D(),    tf.keras.layers.Conv2D(64, (3, 3), activation="relu"),    tf.keras.layers.MaxPooling2D(),    tf.keras.layers.Conv2D(128, (3, 3), activation="relu"),    tf.keras.layers.MaxPooling2D(),    tf.keras.layers.Flatten(),    tf.keras.layers.Dense(128, activation="relu"),    tf.keras.layers.Dropout(0.4),    tf.keras.layers.Dense(1, activation="sigmoid")])model.compile(    optimizer="adam",    loss="binary_crossentropy",    metrics=["accuracy"])model.summary()



Cell 4 — Train the model
history = model.fit(    train_ds,    validation_data=val_ds,    epochs=10)



Cell 5 — Display training accuracy
plt.plot(history.history["accuracy"], label="Training Accuracy")plt.plot(history.history["val_accuracy"], label="Validation Accuracy")plt.xlabel("Epoch")plt.ylabel("Accuracy")plt.title("Fake Currency Detection Model Training")plt.legend()plt.show()



Cell 6 — Save the trained model
model.save("currency_model.keras")print("Model saved successfully!")



You can now connect currency_model.keras to the Streamlit user interface from the previous step.
Important: The model is only as reliable as its dataset. Use genuine and counterfeit images with trustworthy labels, avoid duplicates across training and testing, and test on unseen images. A model prediction is not official verification of a banknote.
















